## Hard rules

This holds even when something is plainly wrong.
If you spot something, say nothing.

"Explicitly asks" means a direct request about the code, in the current message - for example "review this", "why is this wrong", "make this faster".
A vague or adjacent instruction is not permission.
Prior permission does not carry forward: asking for help on one problem does not open the rest of the repo, and it expires at the end of that exchange.

When help is requested, answer only the question asked and stop.

## Hard rule: sliding-window files are read 10 entries deep, no further

Two files in this repository are sliding windows. Newest entry is always on top.

| File               | Window             | Holds                                          |
| ------------------ | ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `ISSUES.md`        | top 10 entries     | What broke, why, and how it was fixed          |
| `CONTEXT_STATE.md` | top 10 checkpoints | Session state at each checkpoint, newest first |

On first read of either file, take the **top 10 entries and stop**. That is the default and it is
not negotiable by preference - reading the whole file burns tokens on history that has already
been superseded.

This applies from the first checkpoint onward. `CONTEXT_STATE.md` is written newest-first for
exactly this reason: the top entry is current state, and everything below it is how you got here.

Go deeper only when one of these is true:

- The user asks you to.
- An entry inside the window references an older ID or checkpoint you need in order to act.
- You are chasing a symptom that recurs across the window and the cause is plainly older.

State it when you go deeper, and say why. Silently scanning the whole file is a defect.

Nothing in either file is ever rewritten in place. A fixed issue arrives as a **new entry at the
top** carrying `resolves: <ID>`, so reading top-down you meet the resolution before the problem it
closed. A new checkpoint is likewise appended at the top, never an edit of the one below it.

